<body>

    <h1 align="center">Twopart</h1>
    <hr size=2>

        <p>Twopart is a cardgame I learned from 4 norwegians I was travelling
          with. I can't even remember if they told me the original name, but if
          they should have done, I forgot it faster as George W. Bush needs to
          say his next bullshit.</p>

        <p>The game is splitted in two parts (hence the name; I'm obviously
          not very imaginative).</p>

        <p>In the first part every player starts with tree cards in his hand
          which they play clockwise.</p>

        <p>After every player (with cards left) put down, the one who
          played the highest (regardless of the colour) gets all of
          them. If there is more than one with the same number (not
          neccessaily the highest), the played pile is not picked up,
          but the people who played the equal cards continue playing
          (according to the rules above) for the whole pile as long as
          there are equal cards. Should there be equal cards, but one of
          the players runs out of cards, he is out and only the others
          can continue. Should all of the people with equal cars run out
          of cards, the one who played the first (equal) cards, gets the
          pile.</p>

        <p>The person picking up the pile starts the next round;
          except if he has no card left, in which case the next player
          having cards continues.</p>

        <p>After playing a card the players get a new one from the staple.
          The colour of the last card of the staple defines the trump for
          part two; this card is demonstrated after the end of the round.</p>

        <p>The first part ends, if no player has cards left anymore. If a player
          didn't collect any cards in the first round, he gets all the
          low cards to (including) 5 from the others; if there are 2 or more
          of such unlucky creatures, they share the stuff (more or less randomly).</p>

        <p>In part two the players try to get rid of the previously collected
          cards.</p>

        <p>A card (or a sequence of cards with the same colour if you select the
          right-most card of the sequence to play) can be played, as long as
          it is bigger (and has the same colour) as the last card on the played
          pile. Alternatively any trump card can be played (of course a bigger
          one, if the last played card is also a trump).</p>

        <p>After completing a round according to that rule, the played pile is
          cleared and the same (the last) player can start a new round.</p>

        <p>If a player can not continue, he must pick up all the cards
          the last player had played.</p>

        <p>The last player having cards left has lost.</p>

        <p>The status line gives some information about what's going on!</p>

        <h2>Note to the layout</h2>
        <p>After starting a game you have (from left to right and from top to
          bottom):</p>

        <ul>
          <li>The staple: A heap of cards (with face down), from where you put
            up new cards (if you put the mouse over it you are informed about
            the number of remaining cards).</li>
          <li>The cards of the human player. The won cards (above) become visible,
            if they are clicked into; right-clicking allows to sort them.</li>
          <li>In the next line it's the same with computer players 1 and 3. In
            between them there's the played pile (which also shows the number of
            the cards inside, is the mouse rests over it).</li>
          <li>In the last line there's computer player 2</li>
        </ul>

    <hr size=2 noshade>
